   {
    "id": "drop",
    "title": "Leaving the pronoun out",
    "blocks": [
     {
      "t": "p",
      "text": "Because the verb ending already shows the person, Bengali very often leaves the pronoun out when it is clear who is meant. You will hear this constantly in speech. Keep the pronoun when you want to stress who is doing it, or when it would otherwise be unclear."
     },
     {
      "t": "ex",
      "title": "No pronoun needed",
      "items": [
       [
        "কোথায় যাচ্ছ?",
        "kothay jachchho?",
        "(Where are you going?)  The verb -ছ shows 'you' (তুমি)."
       ],
       [
        "ভাত খাচ্ছি।",
        "bhat khachchhi.",
        "(I am eating rice.)  The verb -ছি shows 'I'."
       ],
       [
        "বাজারে যাব।",
        "bajare jabo.",
        "(I will go to the market.)  The ending -ব shows 'I'."
       ],
       [
        "কী করছেন?",
        "ki korchhen?",
        "(What are you doing?)  The ending -ছেন shows আপনি."
       ],
       [
        "কাল আসব।",
        "kal ashbo.",
        "(I will come tomorrow.)"
       ],
       [
        "চা খাবে?",
        "cha khabe?",
        "(Will you have tea?)  The ending -বে with a question shows তুমি."
       ]
      ]
     },
     {
      "t": "note",
      "text": "The ending -বে is the same for তুমি and সে (খাবে). When no pronoun is given, context tells you which one is meant."
     }
    ]
   }
